  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nHigh pressure will continue to be in control of our weather over the \nnext week. As mentioned previously, the eastern flank of the ridge \nwill weaken due to a strengthening trough, which will increase \nmoisture into the area. PWATs are anticipated to reach 2\" as \nmoisture pools ahead of a weak surface reflection of this trough. As \nthis moves south, a low (15-25%) chance of showers and thunderstorms \nwill develop Sunday before drier air moves, effectively ending our \nrain chances for the upcoming work week. \n\nTemperatures will remain hot through the upcoming week. Heat index \nvalues will range from 105-109, with isolated locations reaching 110-\n113. While this already sounds miserable for anyone outside, a \nbetter indicator of heat-related impacts is the WetBulb Globe \nTemperature (WBGT), which takes the sun angle, latitude, \ntemperature, sky cover, wind, and moisture into account. For this \nweekend through the upcoming week, WGBT is generally extreme, though \na few locations will \"only\" be high. This means for every hour you \nspend outside, you should take a 20 minute break.",
